    "usecase4_flow_step3_desc": "It disappears from Carlos's phone as soon as it connects.",
    "usecase1_footnote": "* About signing in with Google: Google checks that you're a real person, so you don't need a password. XowApp doesn't store your email address or any other personal data on its servers: to XowApp you're an anonymous identifier, and we have no way of knowing who is behind each account.",
    "about_kicker": "About Us",
    "about_title": "Privacy shouldn't be a luxury",
    "about_photo_alt": "Photo of Raúl Calvo",
    "about_name": "Raúl Calvo",
    "about_role": "Founder and sole developer of XowApp",
    "about_p1": "XowApp has no corporation behind it, no glass offices, and no venture capitalists demanding returns at the expense of your data. I design, code, and maintain it myself, with dedication and care, in the time I can carve out for this personal project.",
    "about_p2": "I am a software developer. I build software for a living, and I created XowApp to solve a problem I experienced firsthand: losing all control over family photos the moment they are sent through social media or apps like WhatsApp.",
    "about_p3": "Being an independent personal project offers a huge advantage: I can build what I truly believe in as a developer. I have no corporate pressure or incentive to hide anything, because I genuinely believe in radical transparency, absolute anonymity, and giving people total ownership over their memories.",
    "about_p4": "It also comes with a practical reality: running servers, cloud infrastructure, and encrypted storage costs real money. I have a family and a full-time job, which is why the transparency page exists: so you can see with total openness what it takes to keep the lights on and how the project is sustained.",
    "about_p5": "How is XowApp funded? Exclusively through voluntary community donations—no intrusive ads, no data harvesting, and no paywalls. I personally cover the entire first year of basic project expenses out of my own pocket to prove my commitment.",
    "about_p6": "I build this because I truly love doing it and because I believe we need an honest alternative where the owner stays in control. If donations cover operational costs, the project will keep growing; if they fall short and I can no longer fund it myself, I will explain it with total transparency. And if donations flourish, every cent will be reinvested into the infrastructure, always honoring our immutable principles of privacy, anonymity, and total owner control.",
    "about_contact_title": "Let's talk",
    "about_contact_body": "If you have questions, ideas, or just want to say hi, you can write to me.",
    "about_contact_email_label": "Email",
    "about_cta_story": "Read why XowApp exists",
    "about_funding_cta": "Want to help out?",
    "historia_kicker": "Why XowApp exists",
    "historia_title": "Why XowApp Was Born",
    "historia_p1": "When my daughter was born, one thing was clear to me from day one: I would decide who saw her photos, and how. Not out of distrust toward anyone in particular, but because once you carry a camera in your pocket everywhere, that decision is no longer automatic—it must be deliberate.",
    "historia_p2": "I had a very concrete reason for being strict. My elderly parents, who were not tech-savvy, had accidentally shared random camera roll photos on Facebook and WhatsApp status multiple times. Nothing grave had happened—until it involved photos of my newborn daughter. That was simply not an option.",
    "historia_p3": "So I did what seemed reasonable at the time: I used WhatsApp's \"view once\" feature, which is supposed to disappear. Every time I sent one, I added the same warning: \"view once, please don't screenshot or forward\". Even though I knew screenshots were still possible.",
    "historia_p4": "Shortly after, I discovered that warning had achieved nothing. There was a mini-trade of screenshot photos of my daughter circulating on WhatsApp among family members behind my back. Worse, some photos reached people outside the family in a chain of trust where each forward came with \"don't show this to anyone\".",
    "historia_p5": "I faced two paths: get angry with my family—who genuinely felt they had done nothing wrong—or accept a more uncomfortable but useful truth: understanding how digital photo sharing actually works.",
    "historia_p6": "When you send a photo on WhatsApp or social media, you are not showing a photo: you are creating a digital copy on a device you do not own. From that moment on, that copy belongs to the other person—and what happens to it depends entirely on their discretion, not yours. It's not malice. It's simply human nature.",
    "historia_p7": "As the owner of that photo—my newborn daughter's photo—the moment I hit send on WhatsApp is the moment I forfeit all control, whether I like it or not, no matter who receives it.",
    "historia_p8": "That thought never left my mind. Being a software developer, I did what I do best: I set out to build the solution rather than just complain. That is how XowApp was born—an app where showing a photo never means losing control of it, where what enters the app never leaves it, and where the owner always stays in control.",
    "historia_pullquote": "\"When you share a photo, you're not showing a memory: you are handing over a digital copy. In XowApp we model showing: the owner always stays in control.\"",
    "vision_kicker": "What we believe",
    "vision_title": "Four ideas we won't change",
    "vision_intro": "These are the principles XowApp is built on, and the reason it exists.",
    "vision_b5_title": "Full commitment to legality *",
    "vision_b5_body": "* Just because we cannot see your content does not make XowApp a haven for illegal activity—quite the opposite. We fully comply with all applicable legal requirements and will cooperate unconditionally with law enforcement whenever required by law.",
